														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1463308">
									

<p>No, I don't know it's name. I don't even know that the coin exists. All I know is that there is this cube, and it's an infohazard. The report doesn't say what it does, and I don't think about it too much because infohazards are nasty. For all I know it creates a chicken every time someone thinks that it's a sphere.</p>
<p>To clarify: SCP-1663 is the cube; the coin doesn't have a designation, because nobody knows that the coin exists.</p>
<p>Also, infohazards that are activated only by their SCP designation stretch my suspension of disbelief somewhat. The designation is not something inherited in the object, it's just a word we use to refer to it. Imagine some poor researcher thinking &quot;Oh, I worked with SCP-2040 last month, and I've read about SCP-2042. I wonder, what SCP-2041 is&quot; and then his heads explodes because 2041 happened to be an infohazard. Most of our existing infohazards wouldn't work if you only know their number: 096 needs you to see his face, 964 can only change what it is, not what other people call it, and 1055 only reacts to fear.</p>
<p>(The fact that your fear 1055 once you know how it works is an example of a non-anomalous memetic effect, by the way. If you are unfamiliar with the concepts of death, pain and danger, then you wouldn't fear him and trigger his growth. But I digress.)</p>
<p>In the case of 1663 it's ambiguous whether knowing the sequence &quot;SCP-Ksi-Lambda-0&quot; triggers the effect, but if SCP-1663 is designation of the cube and the cube only, then keeping the coin in the cube should be reasonably safe either way.</p>

							</div>

								
